.TH CHTTP_BASIC_AUTH_MP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttp_basic_auth_mp \- build an HTTP Basic auth header value with a custom allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttp.h>

char *chttp_basic_auth_mp(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp,
                          const char *username, const char *password);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function builds a "Basic <base64(username:password)>" header
value, as RFC 7617 asks. It makes only the header VALUE. It does not
make the "Authorization: " key part. You can give the result directly
to chttp_request_set_header(req, "authorization", ...) or to the
equivalent function of chttpsvr.

RFC 7617 SS2 makes the colon the one delimiter of the encoded
"user-id:password" string. A recipient cuts that string at its FIRST
colon. A username with a colon in it is therefore not transmittable
without ambiguity, and the function rejects it. Without that
rejection, the username "a:b" with the password "c" encodes to the
same bytes as the username "a" with the password "b:c". A caller that
asks for one identity therefore sends a different one. A colon in the
password needs no escape, and the function builds the string as the
caller asks.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp        A custom allocator, or NULL for malloc/free.
username  The username. It must not be NULL. It can be empty. It must
          not contain a colon (':').
password  The password. It must not be NULL. It can be empty. It can
          contain colons.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A new "Basic <base64>" string, if the call succeeds.
NULL, if the allocation fails, if username or password is NULL, or if
username contains a colon.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttp_basic_auth (3),
.BR chttp_base64_encode_mp (3),
.BR chttp_request_set_header (3)
